A||||9||JohnnyK||No power to starter? Starter not hooked up properly?||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||05-07-2000||04:39 PM||||Just installed a new starter.. The only wires that I hooked up were the big one that goes to the solonoid, and a smaller one.. I did not use a shim if that has anything to do with it, but the problem is when the key is turned, there is nothing. Not even a click. I checked the clutch pedal switch, and it doesn't work when I put it in manually.. Is it possible that the battery is just too discharged? It won't charge anymore for some reason, and the radio and lights work, but thats the only thing I can think off.. It was working before I put a new one in? Thanks<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||JohnnyK||05-07-2000||05:35 PM||||Well, checked the voltage, and it's at 11.8, which I'm assuming would be enough to make the solonoid click... Could it be that without the shim, it is jammed somewhere and doesn't move? But even there, i'm fairly sure there should be a click...? Also, is there a switch or something I can disconnect from that clutch pedal switch thing (thats monitors if the pedal is pushed to the floor) to render it inoperable? (So I can make sure that that isn't the problem, since I did just put a new clutch pedal in)..? Thanks<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ed||05-07-2000||06:46 PM||||You can try using a paper clip to connect the two sides of the safety lock-out switch on your clutch pedal.  This completes a circuit that will allow power to your starter.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||05-07-2000||06:54 PM||||Where can I find these terminals... I think i'll confuse them with the cc..<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Gokart||05-07-2000||08:11 PM||||Could be a blessing that this happened cause it'll give you a chance to PUT THE SHIM BACK!! Or possibly damage the flywheel once you do get it started cause the teeth are not lined up properly.||||reg||4
Z||000005||JohnnyK||05-07-2000||11:58 PM||||Well, it came with 3 really thin shims.. How many should I use? Is this going to be a pain, or can I just remove the two bolts and slip the shim in... None of this engine lifting stuff?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Frank R||05-08-2000||12:44 AM||||  A fully charged battery will reflect 12.6 volts on a digital voltmeter.  A battery showing 11.6 is 80% discharged.  An easy test is turn on the headlites and try the starter.  The lites will go out if the battery is dead.  Frank R...||||reg||6
Z||000007||Gokart||05-08-2000||06:28 AM||||Put the shim back that was there in the close to exact spot where it was. If you threw it out, pick out the new one that closly resembles the old and try to remember which way it was pointing. You do remember seeing the shim when you took out the old starter, right? Just loosen the two bolts that hold up the starter and slide it back in.<p>[This message has been edited by Gokart (edited 05-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||JohnnyK||05-08-2000||08:51 AM||||The old one had a problem of making a grinding noise (Almost like it wasn't disengaging and then being kicked out).. Would that be the starter or the shim space..?||||reg||1
Z||000009||JohnnyK||05-08-2000||01:56 PM||||Well, the neutral start switch thing was not making good contact... But there is a horrible grinding noise.. Think I'm going to take your advice with the shim tonight..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
